WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.890
Narrator: Look at that beard.

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:05.116
The density. The thickness. 

00:00:05.116 --> 00:00:07.640
It's what a beard is supposed to be!

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:08.823
Not this.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.540
This is my beard, if you
even want to call it a beard.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.080
This footage was taken after
I let it grow for two weeks.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.360
Two weeks!

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.640
It's thin, patchy,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.230
and in some spots there's no hair at all.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:24.063
Sad. 

00:00:24.063 --> 00:00:25.996
But let's look at this one again.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.320
This is Kevin.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.830
We're around the same age,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.310
both Caucasian males of Irish descent.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.200
You could say we have a lot in common,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.860
except in the facial hair department.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.590
So, I asked a dermatologist

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.760
about why he's able to
grow a beard and I'm not.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.320
Dr. Chwalek: It's based on
genetics as well as hormones.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.230
Some men, you know, have
more hair follicles,

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.760
so they can grow denser,
coarser beards than others.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.270
Testosterone gets converted
in the hair follicle

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.250
to a more potent form
called dihydrotestosterone.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.510
Some hair follicles have receptors

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.699
on them that are very sensitive

00:00:56.699 --> 00:00:59.200
to this higher form of
testosterone, dihydrotestosterone,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.000
and that will stimulate hair growth.

00:01:02.258 --> 00:01:04.890
Graham: How would you
characterize my facial hair?

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.150
Dr. Chwalek: I'd say it's like, sparse,

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.380
and you have light-colored hair,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.050
so it doesn't show up quite as much.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.700
Graham: I can't really grow a great beard.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.560
It's been a problem.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.430
I want one. I wanna look
like my colleague, Kevin.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.230
I mean, look at that beard.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.650
It is just glorious.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.940
Why can't I grow a beard like Kevin?

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.940
Dr. Chwalek: Well, Kevin
has some good hair genes,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.310
so because of that, the hair follicles

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:26.840
on his face are more sensitive

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.210
to the effects of a form of testosterone

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.280
called dihydrotestosterone which can help

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.480
to stimulate thicker,
coarser, longer hair growth

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.780
in some areas of the
body like the beard area.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.290
Narrator: Beards and facial hair

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:42.290
have long been associated
with masculinity and virility.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.966
I began to wonder if my inability

00:01:43.966 --> 00:01:46.660
to grow thick facial hair like Kevin

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.720
makes me less of a man.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.140
Graham: Does the fact
that I can't grow a beard,

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.490
does it make mean that
I have low testosterone?

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.980
Dr. Chwalek: No. Men
who can't grow a beard

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.210
or have patchy beards

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.280
usually have normal testosterone levels.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.900
It isn't a reflection of
having low testosterone

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.560
or being deficient in testosterone.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.085
Graham: So, you can still be virile -

00:02:05.093 --> 00:02:05.926
Dr. Chwalek: Right.

00:02:05.926 --> 00:02:07.930
Graham: And grow a crappy beard like this?

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:08.763
Dr. Chwalek: Yes.

00:02:08.763 --> 00:02:09.929
Graham: Okay, good.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.330
Narrator: And in fact, there are some ways

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.800
to increase the chances
of growing facial hair,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.480
but shaving more often isn't one of them.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.430
Dr. Chwalek: So, the idea that shaving

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:21.360
can impact hair growth is actually a myth.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.250
When you're shaving, you're
just cutting the hair off

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.910
at the surface of the skin,

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.540
and what's controlling the hair growth

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.400
is really occurring deeper in the skin.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.350
So, it shouldn't have any impact

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.470
on how quickly the hair grows,
or how dense the hair grows.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.610
People have tried topical
Rogaine or Minoxidil.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.830
These things may stimulate
a little hair growth,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.770
but assuming it's due to genetics,

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.720
then you're kind of
stuck with what you have.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.900
Graham: It's all about the
sensitivity of the hair follicles

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.520
and how they interact with this -

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.330
Dr. Chwalek: Dihydrotestosterone
or androgens in the blood.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:51.163
Graham: So, you're saying that,

00:02:51.163 --> 00:02:53.688
that his hair follicles are "sensitive."

00:02:53.688 --> 00:02:54.940
Dr. Chwalek: Yes.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:55.773
Graham: See, mine aren't.

00:02:55.773 --> 00:02:56.606
Dr. Chwalek: Right. 

00:02:56.606 --> 00:02:58.190
Graham: They aren't
sensitive. They're like rocks.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.700
They have no sensitivity at all.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:00.745
Okay, so I win that!

00:03:00.745 --> 00:03:02.070
Dr. Chwalek: Right.

